 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{iters} \cdot N_{rand} \cdot N_{samples} \cdot D)$, where $N_{iters}$ is the number of iterations, $N_{rand}$ is the batch size (rays per step), $N_{samples}$ is the number of samples per ray, and $D$ is the MLP depth/width [S5, S14, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{rand} \cdot N_{samples} \cdot D + \text{Model Size})$. VRAM usage is a critical constraint, often mitigated by chunking rays during the forward pass [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Training typically requires 1,000,000 iterations, which can take several hours to days depending on hardware and scene complexity [S11, S14, unofficial].
-*   **Scaling:** Performance scales with batch size up to VRAM limits; larger batches improve throughput but may reduce generalization if too large [S15, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** End-to-end differentiable, handles complex geometry and appearance, produces high-quality novel views.
-*   **Limitations:** Slow training and rendering, prone to overfitting and "floaters" in empty space, requires accurate camera poses [S1][S17, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** High-quality view synthesis from multi-view images with known poses.
-*   **When Not to Use:** Real-time applications, scenes with unknown camera poses (without pose optimization), or sparse views without regularization.
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{iters} \cdot B \cdot K)$, where $N_{iters}$ is the number of iterations, $B$ is the batch size (number of rays), and $K$ is the number of samples per ray [S5, S14, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(B \cdot K)$ for storing intermediate rendering states per batch, plus $O(N_{rays})$ for pre-computed rays if batching is used [S5, S14, unofficial]. VRAM usage is a primary constraint, often mitigated by chunking rays [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Training typically requires 100,000 to 1,000,000 iterations [S11, S14, unofficial]. For a scene with 199 images, training for 5 epochs with a batch size of 1024 results in ~39,000 iterations [S17, unofficial].
+*   **Scaling:** Performance scales with batch size, but larger batches hinder data loading performance and prevent full GPU occupancy, while smaller batches cap iterations per second [S15, unofficial]. A batch size of $2^{10}$ (1024) is often chosen as a trade-off [S15, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, differentiable end-to-end, produces high-quality novel views [S5, S14, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Slow convergence due to low-frequency bias of MLPs, requires many iterations, sensitive to hyperparameters (learning rate, positional encoding frequencies) [S1][S11, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** When high-quality novel view synthesis is required and computational resources allow for long training times [S5, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When real-time rendering or very fast training is required [S15, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The NeRF Training Loop was introduced in the seminal paper "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S5, S11, unofficial]. It builds on the concept of coordinate-based MLPs for implicit scene representation [S3, unofficial]. The method has become the standard baseline for neural volume rendering, with numerous variants and optimizations developed since its inception [S24]. It is widely used in computer vision and graphics for novel view synthesis, 3D reconstruction, and scene editing [S1, S24].
+The NeRF Training Loop was introduced in the seminal paper "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S5, unofficial]. It established the standard approach of optimizing a coordinate-based MLP to represent a 3D scene by minimizing the photometric error between rendered and observed images. The method has since become the foundation for numerous extensions and variants in neural rendering [S24].
 
